   Thus only the permutation, Temperley–Lieb, and rational orthogonal/symplectic branches remain. The proof uses a third-order expansion and explicit matrix elements. It does not establish the remaining branches’ infrared limits.

4. The mixed graded-permutation chain at zero chemical potential has an exact quadratic excitation branch for either sign of its Hamiltonian. It therefore falls outside the assumed compact-CFT class.

Two proposed shortcuts fail. Nesting rank does not count the full central charge, since fused rank-one models contain additional parafermion degrees of freedom. Also, \(k\le\operatorname{ind}(R)\) bounds only the charged WZW sector; arbitrary neutral spectators require another bound.

The precise missing ingredients are a nearest-neighbor-sensitive upper bound on the general infrared level, and control of all massless TBA sectors for arbitrary regular R matrices.

